Store a new profile, and read its key from a hidden prompt on a terminal, or from stdin otherwise. The key is never a flag, since a flag lands in shell history.

Stdin must hold the key alone. pass show and its siblings print metadata after the secret, so pipe them through head -n 1.

The key goes to the OS keychain, or to profiles.json with --file. An empty key stores a profile with no key when --base-url is not the provider's own. A name is 1 to 64 lowercase letters, digits, - or _, does not start with -, and is not none. The first profile becomes the default.

Usage:
  onesie profile create NAME [flags]

Examples:
  pass show openrouter | head -n 1 | onesie profile create work --provider openrouter
  onesie profile create cf --provider cloudflare --account-id 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef

Flags:
      --account-id ACCOUNT_ID   The account id, 32 hexadecimal characters. Only on
                                cloudflare.
      --allow-http              Store a plain http --base-url to a host other than this
                                machine. Every run on the profile then needs
                                --allow-http too.
      --base-url URL            The API root the profile's runs use.
      --file                    Store the key in profiles.json, not the OS keychain.
  -h, --help                    Show this help.
      --model MODEL             The model a run uses when it names none.
      --provider PROVIDER       The profile's provider: typesafe, openrouter, berget,
                                levanto, cloudflare or vercel. Required.
